bottom-left:
  metric:
    value: "11"
    label: of the 11 warmest years on record fall in 2015 to 2025
bottom-right:
  metric:
    value: "0.21"
    unit: °C
    label: warming per decade from 1975 to 2025, as a straight line through the annual values
rows: 15-9
source: NASA GISS GISTEMP v4, annual land–ocean temperature index
---
title: The three warmest years are the last three
subtitle: Annual anomaly against the 1951–1980 average
left:
  metric:
    value: "1.28"
    unit: °C
    label: 2024, the warmest year of the record
middle:
  metric:
    value: "1.18"
    unit: °C
    label: 2025, the second warmest
right:
  metric:
    value: "1.17"
    unit: °C
    label: 2023, the third warmest
source: NASA GISS GISTEMP v4, annual land–ocean temperature index
---
title: How to read the index
text:
  - It is an anomaly: each year's departure from the 1951–1980 average, in °C. It is not an absolute temperature.
  - It is NASA's land–ocean series, one value for the whole globe for each year.
  - The record starts in 1880 and holds 146 years, to 2025.
  - Values are rounded to 0.01 °C, as NASA publishes them.
---
title: Sources
text:
  - **Data.** NASA GISS Surface Temperature Analysis, GISTEMP v4, global annual land–ocean temperature index ([data.giss.nasa.gov/gistemp](https://data.giss.nasa.gov/gistemp/)), downloaded 9 November 2026. Credit: NASA GISS GISTEMP Team.
  - **Derived.** The twenty-year and decade means, the trend and the counts are worked out from the annual values in `data/global-temperature.csv`.
  - **Baseline.** Every anomaly is against the 1951–1980 average.
